{
  "id": "u03-l06",
  "unit": "u03-family",
  "title": "Calling strangers 'uncle'",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "In Bangladesh you rarely call an adult by name or 'sir/madam'. You **promote them into your family**:\n\n- **ভাই / ভাইয়া**: a man around your age or a bit older (shopkeepers, waiters, colleagues)\n- **আপা / আপু**: a woman a bit older than you\n- **চাচা**: an older man; **খালা**: an older woman\n- **মামা**: rickshaw drivers, tea-stall owners: friendly and informal\n- **আংকেল / আন্টি**: friends' parents and older people in middle-class city life\n\nThese still take **আপনি** (polite verbs) unless you know the person well: *Chacha, apni kemon achhen?*\n\nThree lifesavers: **আমি জানি না** 'I don't know', **বুঝতে পারছি না** 'I don't understand', and **আচ্ছা** 'OK / I see'.",
  "vocab": [
    {
      "id": "apa",
      "bn": "আপা",
      "tr": "apa",
      "en": "elder sister; ma'am",
      "notes": "Respectful address for a woman somewhat older than you — teachers, colleagues, shop customers. A little more formal than আপু."
    },
    {
      "id": "angkel",
      "bn": "আংকেল",
      "tr": "angkel",
      "en": "uncle (address)",
      "notes": "From English 'uncle'; used by urban Bangladeshis for friends' fathers and older men. Also spelled আঙ্কেল."
    },
    {
      "id": "anti",
      "bn": "আন্টি",
      "tr": "anti",
      "en": "auntie (address)",
      "notes": "From English 'auntie'; used for friends' mothers and older women, especially in cities."
    },
    {
      "id": "achchha",
      "bn": "আচ্ছা",
      "tr": "achchha",
      "en": "OK",
      "notes": "Agreement / \"I see\". With a question tone (আচ্ছা?) it means \"Really?\"; at the start of a sentence it can mean \"By the way…\"."
    },
    {
      "id": "jani",
      "bn": "জানি",
      "tr": "jani",
      "en": "(I) know",
      "notes": "From জানা \"know\". আমি জানি না = \"I don't know\"."
    },
    {
      "id": "janen",
      "bn": "জানেন",
      "tr": "janen",
      "en": "(you, polite) know"
    },
    {
      "id": "bujhte-parchhi-na",
      "bn": "বুঝতে পারছি না",
      "tr": "bujhte parchhi na",
      "en": "I don't understand",
      "notes": "Learn as a chunk for now (lit. \"I am not able to understand\"). The পারা \"can\" verb is taught in U8."
    }
  ],
